SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 319 | Wednesday, March 25, 2026

Good morning. Back on time.

1) The FMC votes 4-1 to open a formal inquiry into the container-lessor merger
The Commission wants data on lease rates by trade lane and on the lessors' contracts with the carrier alliances. The companies said they "welcome the review," which is what you say. A decision from the antitrust agencies is still the one that matters; the FMC's inquiry mostly guarantees the paperwork lasts through the year.

2) Q4 GDP revision: inventory investment revised up
Businesses added more stock in the fourth quarter than first estimated. That fits the pattern in every other dataset this month: modest restocking, concentrated in industrial and building-related goods, with retail inventories still lean.

3) A reefer container shortage develops out of Oakland
The early ag season plus the port's new gate hours have pulled more export bookings to Oakland than the carriers positioned reefers for. Two carriers are repositioning empties from LA by rail; one is quoting a $400 reefer surcharge for April. Shippers with contracts that fix equipment availability are fine. Most do not have those.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 6%. The rise in the average price of a new dry van trailer since January, mostly tariffs on the aluminum and steel that go into it.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

